## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of simple concurrent data structures and basic OS synchronization primitives in a beginner-friendly setting.

## Key Concepts
- Bounded buffers, circular queues
- Mutual exclusion, condition signaling
- Producer-consumer correctness, wakeups, fairness
- Deadlock avoidance in bounded channels

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode: bounded mailbox in a tiny OS
class Mailbox {
  constructor(cap) {
    this.buf = new Array(cap);
    this.head = 0; this.tail = 0; this.count = 0; this.cap = cap;
    this.lock = new Mutex();
    this.notEmpty = new CondVar();
    this.notFull = new CondVar();
  }
}

function trySend(mb, m) {
  mb.lock.lock();
  if (mb.count === mb.cap) { mb.lock.unlock(); return false; }
  mb.buf[mb.tail] = m;
  mb.tail = (mb.tail + 1) % mb.cap;
  mb.count++;
  mb.notEmpty.signal();
  mb.lock.unlock();
  return true;
}

function blockingRecv(mb, out) {
  mb.lock.lock();
  while (mb.count === 0) {
    mb.notEmpty.wait(mb.lock);
  }
  *out = mb.buf[mb.head];
  mb.head = (mb.head + 1) % mb.cap;
  mb.count--;
  mb.notFull.signal();
  mb.lock.unlock();
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Stack growth is a core OS feature. This question tests understanding of page faults, per-thread state, synchronization, and safe growth under concurrent faults in a minimal kernel.

## Key Concepts
- Guard pages and dynamic stack growth
- Per-thread stack boundaries (stack_base, stack_limit)
- Atomic/locked updates to stack metadata
- Race conditions when multiple faults occur near the guard
- Basic testing: concurrent faults, growth cap, and resume correctness

## Code Example
```c
// Pseudo-skeleton: handle_stack_fault in a toy OS
void handle_stack_fault(Thread *t, void *fault_addr) {
  if (!in_stack_range(t, fault_addr)) return; // not stack fault
  acquire(&t->stack_lock);
  if (t->stack_limit - t->stack_base < GROW_SIZE) {
    release(&t->stack_lock);
    // terminate or signal OOM
    return;
  }
  map_pages(t, t->stack_limit - GROW_SIZE, GROW_SIZE); // grow downward
  t->stack_limit -= GROW_SIZE;
  release(&t->stack_lock);
  resume_execution(t);
}
```

